"""Original asset-based practice teaching; no proprietary instrument reproduced."""
def build(c,m):
 return c(62,'Asset Based Community Development','Asset Based\nCommunity\nDevelopment','Develop a fictional asset-led action proposal that checks access, consent, resource burdens and evidence limits without substituting community effort for accountable services.',[
 m('Assets needs and unequal access','Distinguish available assets from accessible benefits and unmet needs.',[
'An asset is a potentially useful strength, relationship, skill, place or resource in a particular context. Calling something an asset does not establish who controls it, who can use it or whether it addresses a priority. A hall can be valuable while remaining inaccessible at the times people need it. Begin with people\'s purposes and verify access conditions rather than compiling an impressive inventory. IACD standards connect community strengths with participation and inclusion. Asset-led thinking should therefore sit alongside an honest account of needs, barriers and institutional responsibilities rather than erase them. [S31]',
'The Newham mixed-methods evaluation offers a bounded example of asset-based work in an urban setting involving people with learning disabilities and families. Interviews highlighted relationships, connecting roles and local conditions; the study does not prove that listing assets produces development outcomes. Its interviews took place in 2021 and recruitment involved senior staff, with relatively limited parent perspectives. Published dates in 2025 do not make the observations recent fieldwork. Use the study to ask about connectors, resources and absent perspectives, not to import its programme as a universally effective model. [R41]',
'Access can depend on fees, opening hours, transport, language, permission and how welcoming a space feels. These conditions differ between people and may change. Do not assume that a community-owned resource is automatically shared fairly or that an informal skill is freely available for a project. A fictional asset note can name a resource, its proposed purpose, the access conditions and the person authorised to confirm availability. It should also record whose experience has not been checked. An invented note practises careful judgement; it is not a directory for making real referrals. [S31, S32]',
'An asset approach must not become a reason to withdraw services or blame people for unmet needs. A group can have strong mutual support and still need safe infrastructure, paid expertise or an accountable public response. Ask which proposed contribution is genuinely voluntary and which responsibility belongs elsewhere. Avoid gathering identifying stories, photographing real homes or mapping locations of vulnerable people for coursework. Use the supplied invented setting or another fictional alternative. Distinguishing a potential resource from an available, appropriate and consented contribution is the central learning task, not the size of the final list. [S31, R41]'],
'An invented neighbourhood has a hall, a skilled gardener and a water supply problem. The hall charges a fee, and the gardener has not agreed to volunteer.',
'Prepare three asset notes with purpose, access condition, permission status and one unmet need or external responsibility.',
'Keep the hall fee and unconfirmed volunteer offer visible. Community strengths do not remove the need for an accountable water-service response.', ['S31','R41','S32'],[
('An asset inventory does not establish usable access.','A fictional hall appears on an asset list. What remains to check?','Availability, cost, accessibility and permission for the proposed purpose.',['That its existence proves equal benefit.','That every resident owns it.','That all service needs are solved.'],'A resource is useful only under suitable access conditions.'),
('Strengths and unmet needs can coexist.','A group has strong mutual support but unsafe water. What is defensible?','Recognise the strength and retain the infrastructure responsibility.',['Declare water needs resolved by solidarity.','Blame residents for not volunteering more.','Remove all public responsibilities from the plan.'],'Asset thinking does not erase structural needs.'),
('A named skill is not consent to contribute it.','A gardener is listed as free labour without agreement. What is missing?','A voluntary and bounded agreement about any contribution.',['A public personal history.','A rule making all skills compulsory.','A claim that mapping creates consent.'],'Availability and consent require separate confirmation.'),
('Publication dates differ from fieldwork dates.','A 2025 ABCD paper reports 2021 interviews. Which date describes the interviews?','2021.',['2025 because the paper is recent.','2026 because it was searched now.','The year of the learner\'s certificate.'],'Publication and data collection dates serve different purposes.'),
('An evaluation in one setting is not universal proof.','A learner says the Newham evaluation proves every asset map improves outcomes. What is wrong?','Its design and context do not establish that general causal claim.',['All qualitative evidence is worthless.','A title guarantees transportability.','Every listed asset was tested in Nigeria.'],'Contextual evidence informs questions without universal causal conclusions.')]),
 m('Connections contributions and burdens','Plan reciprocal connections with voluntary contributions and realistic resourcing.',[
'Connections may make a resource more usable: a trusted introduction, an accessible timetable or an agreed way to share equipment can matter more than another inventory entry. The person connecting groups needs time, competence, support and a clear role. The Newham evaluation describes connecting work and the importance of local relationships, while also making resource demands visible. Its formative findings are not a staffing formula for a Nigerian programme. Specify a proposed connector role in plain terms, including what it may arrange, what requires approval and how the person can seek support. [R41]',
'Reciprocity means considering what people offer and receive without treating every relationship as a transaction. A contribution must be voluntary, informed and feasible; declining it should not automatically exclude someone from unrelated benefits. Check the time, travel and opportunity costs as well as money. A household with insecure work may face a much greater burden from an unpaid meeting than a salaried organiser. IACD inclusion principles make these differences relevant, and participation guidance supports planning the resources needed for access. Do not assume that a contribution is fair because its cash value is small. [S31, S32]',
'The 2026 Tanzanian qualitative study examines how programme design and competition for resources may interact with local collective action. Interviews and observations from 2019-20 raise questions about upfront work, uncertain funding and expectations. They do not establish a universal rule that competition always damages cooperation. Government and NGO activities provided different goods and operated under different trust conditions, limiting causal comparisons. For a fictional proposal, explain the work requested before funding is confirmed and what happens if funding fails. Avoid promising later reimbursement or benefits that no authority has approved. [R43]',
'An agreement about shared activity should identify purpose, bounded responsibilities, access conditions, resources and a way to revisit the arrangement. Do not publish a real volunteer contact list or collect evidence of hardship in class to judge who deserves support. Work with invented roles and aggregate assumptions, and mark availability as unverified where necessary. If an activity depends on donated expertise, include the consequence of its withdrawal and an authorised alternative. An informal connection can be valuable, but it is not a guaranteed service, a substitute for safeguarding or proof that a community can absorb unlimited unpaid work. [S31, R41]'],
'A fictional project requests three unpaid workdays before a grant decision and promises every contributor a later benefit. A connector has no allocated time.',
'Write a contribution agreement outline with voluntary scope, burdens, uncertain funding, connector support and a withdrawal contingency.',
'Remove the unapproved guarantee. Make the cost of participation and grant uncertainty explicit, and provide a realistic alternative if contributions are unavailable.', ['R41','S31','S32','R43'],[
('Connecting work needs allocated resources.','A fictional connector is assigned daily tasks with no time or support. What needs planning?','Feasible workload, support and role boundaries.',['A claim that goodwill removes all costs.','An assumption of unlimited volunteer capacity.','An unrelated outcome guarantee.'],'Relationships require practical work and support.'),
('Equal requested time can impose unequal burdens.','Two residents are asked for the same unpaid day. What should be considered?','Different opportunity costs and whether participation is genuinely feasible.',['That identical hours prove fairness.','That low income means automatic consent.','That travel time cannot matter.'],'Participation costs differ between people.'),
('Funding uncertainty should be disclosed before work is requested.','A grant is undecided but benefits are promised for upfront labour. What correction is needed?','Explain uncertainty and avoid guarantees beyond approved authority.',['Hide the funding status.','Record the grant as received.','Make unpaid work compulsory.'],'People need honest information before choosing a contribution.'),
('Qualitative comparisons have alternative explanations.','Government and NGO projects deliver different goods. What limits a causal comparison?','Differences in goods, actors and prior trust may influence accounts.',['The title proves identical conditions.','Publication cancels confounding.','All competition must therefore be prohibited.'],'Design and context affect interpretation.'),
('Voluntary contributions need withdrawal contingencies.','A fictional expert can no longer donate time. What improves the proposal?','An authorised alternative or revised feasible scope.',['Assume the promise cannot change.','Expose the expert\'s private circumstances.','Pretend the work was completed.'],'A plan should remain honest when resources change.')]),
 m('Asset-led action and learning','Review an asset-led proposal using access and outcome distinctions.',[
'An asset-led proposal should connect a community-defined purpose to a small feasible action rather than leap from a resource list to a promised transformation. Name the intended users, the access conditions, the responsible roles and the assumptions about how the activity might help. A fictional shared garden may offer a learning opportunity, but the presence of land and volunteers does not prove food security or equitable benefit. IACD standards support collective learning and planning; the learner should distinguish this introductory planning task from advanced research design or an approved professional placement. [S31]',
'Use more than one kind of review question. An activity measure can show whether an agreed session happened; an access question can examine who could use it; an outcome question asks what changed for the intended people. Keep the units, time period and missing information clear. More listed assets or more volunteer hours may reflect effort, unequal burdens or improved recording rather than better development outcomes. The Newham evaluation includes cost analysis but does not establish cost-effectiveness without suitable outcome comparison. Avoid assigning an invented monetary benefit to every social connection to make a proposal look successful. [R41]',
'Contextual Nigerian evidence can prompt questions about organisation resources and representation, but it must be appraised carefully. The Rivers State youth-organisation survey uses officials from selected registered organisations, not a statewide random sample of every young person. Its reporting inconsistencies and potentially overlapping companion paper prevent treating the two publications as independent confirmation of effects. Do not teach its numerical associations as robust causal findings. In a fictional review, ask whose voice the organisation record misses and what additional authorised feedback would be needed before generalising. [R44]',
'Learning from a proposal includes identifying what should stop, change or remain uncertain. Assign an owner and a review point, and distinguish a recommendation from a decision already made. A group may choose not to proceed when the burden or access conditions are unacceptable. That can be a responsible planning result rather than a failure of community motivation. Keep real household details, locations and volunteer records out of learner resources. The completed worksheet demonstrates reasoning within an invented setting; actual implementation, supervised skill observation, local practice review and academic approval remain separate requirements. [S31, S32]'],
'A fictional garden lists twenty assets and eighty volunteer hours. The organiser claims proven food security, although access times exclude shift workers and no outcomes were measured.',
'Prepare a review separating activity, access, burden and outcome questions, plus one justified revision and its owner.',
'The counts show recorded resources and effort. Address excluded users and burdens, retain outcome uncertainty and assign a feasible revision without inventing impact.', ['S31','R41','R44','S32'],[
('Asset counts are not development outcomes.','A fictional list grows from ten to twenty assets. What can it establish alone?','A larger recorded inventory, not proven benefit.',['A doubled food-security outcome.','Causal inclusion of every resident.','Automatic programme effectiveness.'],'Inventory size and benefit are different measures.'),
('Cost analysis is not cost-effectiveness.','A project calculates staff and volunteer costs without comparative outcomes. What is missing for cost-effectiveness?','Appropriate outcome information and a defensible comparison.',['Only a more attractive currency symbol.','An assumption that all donations are free.','A larger attendance list.'],'Costs alone do not establish benefit relative to alternatives.'),
('Officials do not represent all youth experiences automatically.','A survey samples registered organisation officials. What generalisation needs caution?','Claims about all young people statewide.',['The fact that officials responded.','The existence of selected organisations.','The need to identify the sampling frame.'],'The sampling frame excludes many perspectives.'),
('Related papers may share data.','Two papers use the same author, frame and response counts. What is appropriate?','Flag possible overlap and avoid calling them independent replications.',['Count them as two independent experiments.','Assume publication proves distinct samples.','Teach their associations as causal effects.'],'Dataset independence must be established, not assumed.'),
('A responsible revision may reduce or stop an activity.','Access barriers and burdens remain unacceptable. What can a review recommend?','A justified pause or smaller feasible scope with a decision owner.',['Proceed because all assets must be used.','Hide the barriers from reviewers.','Claim completed professional competence.'],'Learning includes deciding when a proposal is unsuitable.')])])
